</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Practical Implementation of <span id="S5.SS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LITE</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present practical execution strategies for implementing <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LITE</span> (<a href="#alg1" title="In 5.1 Acceleration Methods from the ODE Perspective ‣ 5 The LITE Approach ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) in LLM-pretraining. Our approach specifically targets the anisotropic landscape inherent to each parameter block. The core objective is to efficiently identify the flat (or sharp) directions within each parameter block.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Efficiently approximating the flat directions</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Martens, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Morwani et al., 2025</a>)</cite> demonstrates that the Fisher matrix and Shampoo-like preconditioners effectively approximates the Gauss-Newton component, the dominant term in the Hessian. Since Muon and SOAP adopt Shampoo-like methods to approximate the square root of the Fisher matrix via Kronecker factorization, we hypothesize that <em id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">the top eigenspaces of their preconditioners and the Hessian are highly aligned. </em></p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22681v1/model.layers.1.mlp.up_proj.weight.svg" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/154;" width="476" height="154"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Coverage of the top eigenspaces of row (column) Hessians by those of <math id="S5.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\top}G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\top}G</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S5.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GG^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GG^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>) for the <span id="S5.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">up_proj</span> block of an FFN layer. A higher score indicates a greater degree of containment. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To verify this hypothesis, we conducted experiments on a toy LLaMA-2 model (see experimental details in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Experimental Details in Sections ‣ Appendix C Experimental Details ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>). <a href="#S2.F2" title="In Training Dynamics and Loss Landscape in Deep Learning. ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (left) confirms that the landscape within each block is ill-conditioned, with sharp directions accounting for only a small proportion of the spectrum. Besides, Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Efficiently approximating the flat directions ‣ 5.2 Practical Implementation of LITE ‣ 5 The LITE Approach ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> indicate that for a small dimension <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{s}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{s}=4</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4/32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4/32</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4/16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4/16</annotation></semantics></math>), the top-<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspaces of the Hessian (for rows/columns) can be effectively covered by a subspace of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\top}G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\top}G</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GG^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GG^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>) of dimension only slightly larger than <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the stochastic gradient matrix. Consequently, by taking the orthogonal complement, we can obtain a reliable approximation of the flat directions. Since preconditioners of Muon and SOAP are constructed from <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\top}G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\top}G</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GG^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GG^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>), we propose the following acceleration schemes tailored for them.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Accelerating Muon (<span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#800080;">Muon-LITE</span>)</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At iteration <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq n</annotation></semantics></math>) represent the stochastic gradient for a given parameter block. The update process begins with the momentum accumulation <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{k}=(1-\alpha)M_{k-1}+G_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{k}=(1-\alpha)M_{k-1}+G_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, followed by the Nesterov correction <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}_{k}=M_{k}+G_{k}/(1-\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}_{k}=M_{k}+G_{k}/(1-\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              We adopt the Nesterov formulation consistent with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jordan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</span></span></span>. Recognizing that <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}_{k}^{\top}\widetilde{M}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}_{k}^{\top}\widetilde{M}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> acts as a robust proxy for the expected covariance <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[G_{k}^{\top}G_{k}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[G_{k}^{\top}G_{k}]</annotation></semantics></math> (Appendix <a href="#A9.SS1" title="I.1 Exact Preconditioner (Riemannian Metrics) Forms for Common Adaptive Optimizers ‣ Appendix I Some Common Adaptive Optimizers for LLM Pre-training ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I.1</span></a>), we utilize it to characterize the landscape geometry. Specifically, we construct the projection matrix <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> onto the top-<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspace of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}_{k}^{\top}\widetilde{M}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}_{k}^{\top}\widetilde{M}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> specifies the dimension of the sharp subspace. A numerically efficient, optimizer state-free method for estimating <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is detailed in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Details of Muon-LITE ‣ Appendix B Algorithm Details ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>. Consequently, the update direction is formulated as:</p>
<table id="S5.E5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S5.E5X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E5X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle U_{k}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle U_{k}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E5X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widetilde{M}_{k}(\widetilde{M}_{k}^{\top}\widetilde{M}_{k})^{-\frac{1}{2}}(P_{k}+\chi(I_{n}-P_{k}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widetilde{M}_{k}(\widetilde{M}_{k}^{\top}\widetilde{M}_{k})^{-\frac{1}{2}}(P_{k}+\chi(I_{n}-P_{k}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.E5Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E5Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+G_{k}(G_{k}^{\top}G_{k})^{-\frac{1}{2}}(\beta_{1}P_{k}+\chi\beta_{2}(I_{n}-P_{k}))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+G_{k}(G_{k}^{\top}G_{k})^{-\frac{1}{2}}(\beta_{1}P_{k}+\chi\beta_{2}(I_{n}-P_{k})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}\geq\beta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}\geq\beta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are the Hessian damping coefficients, and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the amplification ratio of the learning rate in flat directions relative to sharp ones. Setting <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1,2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1,2}=0</annotation></semantics></math> recovers the original Muon baseline. Here we use the precondition <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G_{k}^{\top}G_{k})^{-\frac{1}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G_{k}^{\top}G_{k})^{-\frac{1}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widetilde{M}_{k}^{\top}\widetilde{M}_{k})^{-\frac{1}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widetilde{M}_{k}^{\top}\widetilde{M}_{k})^{-\frac{1}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the gradient term, since it has superior numerical stability and efficiency of applying NS iterations, and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{k}^{\top}G_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{k}^{\top}G_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> itself serves as a valid instantaneous estimator of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[G_{k}^{\top}G_{k}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[G_{k}^{\top}G_{k}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Accelerating SOAP (<span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#800080;">SOAP-LITE</span>)</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We retain the notation established above. As detailed in <a href="#S4.T1" title="In 4.1 A Unified Discrete Formulation ‣ 4 A Unified Riemannian ODE Framework for Understanding Adaptive Algorithms ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the SOAP preconditioner explicitly provides the eigenvalues (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>) and eigenvectors (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{r}\otimes Q_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{r}\otimes Q_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>) of the curvature estimation. Consequently, we can identify the sharp directions directly in the (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{r}\otimes Q_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{r}\otimes Q_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>)-rotated eigenspace by selecting the top-<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{s}\leq mn" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{s}\leq mn</annotation></semantics></math>) elements of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the mask matrix indicating the sharp directions at iteration <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k}=1_{m}1_{n}^{\top}-P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k}=1_{m}1_{n}^{\top}-P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the flat directions. The update direction for accelerating SOAP is given by:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex5X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex5X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle U_{k}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle U_{k}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex5X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q_{l}\left(V_{k}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\odot(\beta_{1}P_{k}+\chi\beta_{2}Q_{k})\odot(Q_{l}^{\top}G_{k}Q_{r})\right)Q_{r}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q_{l}\left(V_{k}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\odot(\beta_{1}P_{k}+\chi\beta_{2}Q_{k})\odot(Q_{l}^{\top}G_{k}Q_{r})\right)Q_{r}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex5Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex5Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+Q_{l}\left(V_{k}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\odot(P_{k}+\chi Q_{k})\odot(Q_{l}^{\top}M_{k}Q_{r})\right)Q_{r}^{\top}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+Q_{l}\left(V_{k}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\odot(P_{k}+\chi Q_{k})\odot(Q_{l}^{\top}M_{k}Q_{r})\right)Q_{r}^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where, similar to <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Muon-LITE</span>, the SOAP baseline is recovered when <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1,2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1,2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
In practice, to enhance training stability, we implement <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> using a soft transition scheme (smoothly changing from 0 to 1) rather than a hard binary cutoff. See Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Details of SOAP-LITE ‣ Appendix B Algorithm Details ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a> for implementation details.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LITE</span> in accelerating Muon and SOAP on a wide range of LLM pre-training scenarios. Experimental details are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Algorithm Details ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> and <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Experimental Details ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Results on Dense Models</h3>

<figure id="S6.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22681v1/muon_130m.svg" id="S6.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:115/114;" width="115" height="114"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22681v1/muon_250m.svg" id="S6.F4.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:115/114;" width="115" height="114"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22681v1/muon_500m.svg" id="S6.F4.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:115/115;" width="115" height="115"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22681v1/muon_1b.svg" id="S6.F4.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:115/115;" width="115" height="115"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S6.F4.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance comparison of Muon and <span id="S6.F4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Muon-LITE</span> on LLM pre-training tasks. We evaluate LLaMA2 models across various sizes and datasets using a <span id="S6.F4.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cos</span> learning rate schedule. The suffixes L and H denote two ablation variants of LITE: in flat directions, L increases only the learning rate ratio <math id="S6.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, while H increases only the Hessian damping coefficient <math id="S6.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The token batch size is approximately 2M for the 1.3B model, and 1M for others.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Main Results.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S6.F4" title="In 6.1 Results on Dense Models ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figures</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Main Results. ‣ 6.1 Results on Dense Models ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> compare the performance of <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Muon-LITE</span> and <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SOAP-LITE</span> against vanilla Muon and SOAP across a range of LLaMA model sizes on both the C4 and Pile datasets. The values of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\chi,\beta_{1,2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\chi,\beta_{1,2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> in <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LITE</span> were determined via a search on the 0.25B model experiments and were then used uniformly for other experiments. Across all experimental settings, <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Muon-LITE</span> and <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SOAP-LITE</span> consistently achieve lower terminal losses than their respective well-tuned baselines. Besides, <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LITE</span> outperforms both <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LITE</span>-L and <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LITE</span>-H, validating the effectiveness of simultaneously increasing <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. To further assess scalability, we visualize the scaling laws of <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Muon-LITE</span> versus Muon in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 3rd item ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (right). The performance gains yielded by <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Muon-LITE</span> remain consistent across a wide range of model scales, indicating its potential scalability. Further downstream analyses are provided in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Additional Experimental Results ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We subsequently investigate the acceleration efficacy of <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LITE</span> over extended training horizons, increasing the training token budget from <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="40\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40\times</annotation></semantics></math> (10k iterations) the parameter count to <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="100\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\times</annotation></semantics></math> (25k) and <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="200\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>200</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200\times</annotation></semantics></math> (50k). <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 3rd item ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (left) indicates that the loss reduction gains yielded by <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Muon-LITE</span> persist across these extended regimes. Notably, at the <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="100\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\times</annotation></semantics></math> setting, <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Muon-LITE</span> achieves an approximate <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup over Muon. This demonstrates the sustained scalability of <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Muon-LITE</span> with respect to the number of training tokens.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22681v1/soap_250m.svg" id="S6.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:229/228;" width="229" height="228"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22681v1/soap_500m.svg" id="S6.F5.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:229/229;" width="229" height="229"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S6.F5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance comparison of SOAP and <span id="S6.F5.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SOAP-LITE</span> on LLM pre-training tasks. Same experimental setup and notations are employed as in <a href="#S6.F4" title="In 6.1 Results on Dense Models ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Ablation Studies.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate that the acceleration strategy for flat directions is detrimental when applied to sharp directions. Under the experimental setting for the 0.25B model in <a href="#S6.F4" title="In 6.1 Results on Dense Models ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we tested applying uniform damping coefficients <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1,2}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1,2}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1,2}=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1,2}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>. Despite these values being smaller than the coefficient used for flat directions in <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Muon-LITE</span>-H (<math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}=2.0</annotation></semantics></math>), they yielded terminal losses of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.113" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.113</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.113</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.129" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.129</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.129</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, both even inferior to the vanilla Muon baseline (<math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.110" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.110</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.110</annotation></semantics></math>). The results validate our design choice of maintaining hyper-parameters along sharp directions when selectively accelerating the flat ones.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Results on MoE Models</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mixture-of-experts (MoE) architectures have emerged as a critical paradigm for modern LLMs. By selectively activating a subset of experts for each token, MoE models achieve superior scaling efficiency over their dense counterparts.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F6" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatleft" style="width:23%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22681v1/qwen2_1b.svg" id="S6.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:112/112;" width="112" height="112"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S6.F6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">Muon-LITE<span id="S6.F6.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> outperforms Muon in QwenMoE pre-training tasks.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Muon-LITE</span> against the Muon baseline on a 1B-parameter QwenMoE model, utilizing a <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">wsd</span> learning rate schedule. The results demonstrate that <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Muon-LITE</span> consistently outperforms Muon throughout the entire training trajectory, ultimately achieving a significantly lower terminal loss (<a href="#S6.F6" title="In 6.2 Results on MoE Models ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). Furthermore, we observe that the performance gap between <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Muon-LITE</span> and Muon progressively widens as iterations proceed during the stable phase. This trend suggests that the efficiency gains offered by <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Muon-LITE</span> are likely to persist or even amplify at larger data scales.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Theoretical Analysis to Training Dynamics</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide a theoretical characterization of the acceleration mechanism of <span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LITE</span>. We adopt and extend the <em id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">River-Valley</em> landscape framework recently proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which models the pre-training loss landscape as a deep valley with a flat river at its bottom. We <span id="S7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">generalize this framework</span> to high-dimensional settings, and <span id="S7.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">analyze the dynamics</span> (<a href="#S5.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 5.1 Acceleration Methods from the ODE Perspective ‣ 5 The LITE Approach ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) corresponding to <span id="S7.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LITE</span> (<a href="#alg1" title="In 5.1 Acceleration Methods from the ODE Perspective ‣ 5 The LITE Approach ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Our analysis demonstrates that <span id="S7.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LITE</span> effectively accelerates the training dynamics along the River, which dictates the terminal loss. In the following, we introduce the assumptions required for our theoretical derivation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 7.1</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(River Structure)<span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fix an integer <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{s}\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{s}\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and an open set <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset\mathbb{R}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset\mathbb{R}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{s}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{s}(w)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the Euclidean projection onto the top <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspace of the Hessian <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}f(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}f(w)</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume that <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{s}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{s}(w)</annotation></semantics></math> varies smoothly with respect to <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, and that the set (referred to as <em id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">River</em>)</p>
<table id="S7.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{R}=\{w\in U:P_{s}(w)\nabla f(w)=0\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}=\{w\in U:P_{s}(w)\nabla f(w)=0\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">constitutes a <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p-d_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p-d_{s})</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional manifold. Furthermore, we assume that within the sharp subspace <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Range}(P_{s}(w))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Range</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Range}(P_{s}(w))</annotation></semantics></math>, the Hessian <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}f(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}f(w)</annotation></semantics></math> and the preconditioner <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(w)</annotation></semantics></math> share a common eigen-basis. Finally, the ODE</p>
<table id="S7.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{d}{dt}\psi_{t}(w)=-P_{s}(\psi_{t}(w))F^{-1}(\psi_{t}(w))\nabla f(\psi_{t}(w))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{dt}\psi_{t}(w)=-P_{s}(\psi_{t}(w))F^{-1}(\psi_{t}(w))\nabla f(\psi_{t}(w)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">initialized at any <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{0}(w)=w\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{0}(w)=w\in U</annotation></semantics></math> is assumed to remain and converge within <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Under Assumption <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption 7.1 (River Structure). ‣ 7 Theoretical Analysis to Training Dynamics ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>, we define the “projection” map to River <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:U\to\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:U\to\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> via the limit of the flow: <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(w)\coloneqq\lim_{t\to\infty}\psi_{t}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≔</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(w)\coloneqq\lim_{t\to\infty}\psi_{t}(w)</annotation></semantics></math>. This ensures <math id="S7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(w)\in\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(w)\in\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> since the stationarity of the flow implies <math id="S7.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P_{s}\nabla f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{s}\nabla f=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 7.2</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Regularity, Smoothness and Conservation)<span id="S7.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the domain <math id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> in Assumption <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption 7.1 (River Structure). ‣ 7 Theoretical Analysis to Training Dynamics ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>, we assume:</p>
<ol id="S7.I1" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1em;">
<li id="S7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is analytic in <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\cdot)|_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\cdot)|_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the restriction of an operator to the sharp subspace <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Range}(P_{s}(w))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Range</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Range}(P_{s}(w))</annotation></semantics></math>. For all <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in U</annotation></semantics></math>, the eigenvalues of <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}f(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}f(w)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(w)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(w)|_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(w)|_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F^{-\frac{1}{2}}(w)\nabla^{2}f(w)F^{-\frac{1}{2}}(w))|_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F^{-\frac{1}{2}}(w)\nabla^{2}f(w)F^{-\frac{1}{2}}(w))|_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> lie in <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-L,L]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-L,L]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\lambda_{F},\rho]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\lambda_{F},\rho]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\lambda_{F_{s}},\rho]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\lambda_{F_{s}},\rho]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\lambda_{H_{F},s},L_{H_{F},s}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\lambda_{H_{F},s},L_{H_{F},s}]</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exist constants <math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta,\delta_{F},\varepsilon\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta,\delta_{F},\varepsilon\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\{\delta,\delta_{F}\}\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\{\delta,\delta_{F}\}\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, such that for any <math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in U</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla f(w)\|_{F(w)^{-1}}\leq G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla f(w)\|_{F(w)^{-1}}\leq G</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla P_{s}(w)\|_{\mathrm{op}}\leq{\delta}/{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>op</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla P_{s}(w)\|_{\mathrm{op}}\leq{\delta}/{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla F(w)\|_{\mathrm{op}}\leq\delta_{F}/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>op</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla F(w)\|_{\mathrm{op}}\leq\delta_{F}/G</annotation></semantics></math>.
See (<a href="#A10.E75" title="Equation 75 ‣ Notations ‣ Appendix J Proof ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">75</span></a>) for detailed definition of the operator norm <math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{\mathrm{op}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>op</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{\mathrm{op}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exists a time horizon <math id="S7.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{max}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{max}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, dependent on the hyper-parameters <math id="S7.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\beta_{1},\{\eta_{t}\}_{t\geq 0},\chi,\beta_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\beta_{1},\{\eta_{t}\}_{t\geq 0},\chi,\beta_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, such that for any initialization <math id="S7.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{0}\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{0}\in U</annotation></semantics></math> with initial momentum <math id="S7.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{0}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the trajectory <math id="S7.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by the dynamics (<a href="#S5.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 5.1 Acceleration Methods from the ODE Perspective ‣ 5 The LITE Approach ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) remains strictly within <math id="S7.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S7.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,T_{\text{max}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,T_{\text{max}}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.3</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose Assumptions <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption 7.1 (River Structure). ‣ 7 Theoretical Analysis to Training Dynamics ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a> and <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem2" title="Assumption 7.2 (Regularity, Smoothness and Conservation). ‣ 7 Theoretical Analysis to Training Dynamics ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a> hold. Define the decay rate <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{t}=\min\{\alpha/2,\lambda_{H_{F,s}}\beta_{1}\eta_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{t}=\min\{\alpha/2,\lambda_{H_{F,s}}\beta_{1}\eta_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and the forgetting kernel <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="K(t)=\exp\left(-\int_{0}^{t}\iota_{s}ds\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(t)=\exp\left(-\int_{0}^{t}\iota_{s}ds\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assuming <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\eta}_{t}\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\eta}_{t}\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a constant <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> (dependent on <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and the hyper-parameters in (<a href="#S5.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 5.1 Acceleration Methods from the ODE Perspective ‣ 5 The LITE Approach ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>)) such that for all <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\leq\varepsilon_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\leq\varepsilon_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the trajectory <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> governed by (<a href="#S5.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 5.1 Acceleration Methods from the ODE Perspective ‣ 5 The LITE Approach ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) satisfies the attraction bound:</span></p>
<table id="S7.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|w_{t}-\Phi(w_{t})\|_{2}^{2}\lesssim K(t)+\varepsilon," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≲</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|w_{t}-\Phi(w_{t})\|_{2}^{2}\lesssim K(t)+\varepsilon,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and the “projected” trajectory <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}=\Phi(w_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}=\Phi(w_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> follows the coupled dynamics:</span></p>
<table id="S7.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{cases}\!\dot{z}_{t}\!=\!-\eta_{t}\!\!\left(\chi P_{\mathcal{R}}(z_{t})F(z_{t})^{-1}(s_{t}\!+\!\beta_{2}\!\nabla f(z_{t}))\!+\!\epsilon_{z,t}\right)\!\!,\\
\!\dot{s}_{t}\!=\!-\alpha s_{t}\!+\!\nabla f(z_{t}),\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:0.608em;" width="0.608em"><mo>=</mo></mpadded><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>χ</mi><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.052em">+</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mpadded style="width:0.049em;" width="0.049em"><mo>)</mo></mpadded><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.052em">+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{cases}\!\dot{z}_{t}\!=\!-\eta_{t}\!\!\left(\chi P_{\mathcal{R}}(z_{t})F(z_{t})^{-1}(s_{t}\!+\!\beta_{2}\!\nabla f(z_{t}))\!+\!\epsilon_{z,t}\right)\!\!,\\
\!\dot{s}_{t}\!=\!-\alpha s_{t}\!+\!\nabla f(z_{t}),\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{z,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{z,t}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a perturbation term. Finally, defining <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="b_{t}=\|P_{\mathcal{R}}(z_{t})F(z_{t})^{-1}\nabla f(z_{t})\|_{F(z_{t})}^{2}+\|P_{\mathcal{R}}(z_{t})F(z_{t})^{-1}s_{t}\|_{F(z_{t})}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{t}=\|P_{\mathcal{R}}(z_{t})F(z_{t})^{-1}\nabla f(z_{t})\|_{F(z_{t})}^{2}+\|P_{\mathcal{R}}(z_{t})F(z_{t})^{-1}s_{t}\|_{F(z_{t})}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the perturbation error satisfies:</span></p>
<table id="S7.E10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S7.E10X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E10X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|\epsilon_{z,t}\|_{F(z_{t})}^{2}\lesssim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≲</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|\epsilon_{z,t}\|_{F(z_{t})}^{2}\lesssim</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E10X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K(t)+\varepsilon^{2}b_{t}+\varepsilon\int_{0}^{t}K(\tau-t)b_{\tau}d\tau." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">d</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K(t)+\varepsilon^{2}b_{t}+\varepsilon\int_{0}^{t}K(\tau-t)b_{\tau}d\tau.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is in Lemmas <a href="#A10.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma J.6. ‣ J.2 Dynamics Analysis ‣ Appendix J Proof ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J.6</span></a> and <a href="#A10.Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma J.11. ‣ J.2 Dynamics Analysis ‣ Appendix J Proof ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J.11</span></a>. We treat <math id="S7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> as a small constant to enforce a slowly varying <math id="S7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Attouch et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, we can take <math id="S7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}\equiv\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}\equiv\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=2\lambda_{F_{H,s}}^{1/2}\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=2\lambda_{F_{H,s}}^{1/2}\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\beta_{1}=\lambda_{F_{H,s}}^{-1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=\lambda_{F_{H,s}}^{-1/2}</annotation></semantics></math> to achieve a fast decay <math id="S7.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(t)=\exp(-\eta\lambda_{F_{H,s}}^{1/2}t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(t)=\exp(-\eta\lambda_{F_{H,s}}^{1/2}t)</annotation></semantics></math> (<em id="S7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em> fast convergence on sharp directions).
In this case, Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 7.3. ‣ 7 Theoretical Analysis to Training Dynamics ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.3</span></a> indicates that the dynamics (<a href="#S5.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 5.1 Acceleration Methods from the ODE Perspective ‣ 5 The LITE Approach ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) first converge to the <math id="S7.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{1/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon^{1/2})</annotation></semantics></math> neighborhood of <math id="S7.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> after a <math id="S7.p3.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Theta(\eta^{-1}\lambda_{F_{H,s}}^{-1/2}\log(\varepsilon^{-1}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(\eta^{-1}\lambda_{F_{H,s}}^{-1/2}\log(\varepsilon^{-1}))</annotation></semantics></math> interval, then faithfully track the decoupled acceleration dynamics on <math id="S7.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> closely, where the tracking error exhibits a fading memory property, driven predominantly by the immediate history of the underlying dynamics. Next we clarify how <math id="S7.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> influence the convergence of the decoupled dynamics in <math id="S7.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the detailed proof is in Lemma <a href="#A10.Thmtheorem12" title="Lemma J.12. ‣ J.2 Dynamics Analysis ‣ Appendix J Proof ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J.12</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.4</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose Assumptions <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption 7.1 (River Structure). ‣ 7 Theoretical Analysis to Training Dynamics ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>, <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem2" title="Assumption 7.2 (Regularity, Smoothness and Conservation). ‣ 7 Theoretical Analysis to Training Dynamics ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a> hold and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\eta}_{t}\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\eta}_{t}\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{f}=f(z_{0})-\inf f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{f}=f(z_{0})-\inf f</annotation></semantics></math>. For the unperturbed dynamics in (<a href="#S7.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Theorem 7.3. ‣ 7 Theoretical Analysis to Training Dynamics ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) with <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{z,t}\equiv 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{z,t}\equiv 0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a constant <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (depending on <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and hyper-parameters in (<a href="#S5.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 5.1 Acceleration Methods from the ODE Perspective ‣ 5 The LITE Approach ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>)), such that for any <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\leq\varepsilon_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\leq\varepsilon_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\leq T_{\mathrm{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\leq T_{\mathrm{max}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the trajectory <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S7.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\int_{0}^{T}\eta_{t}\|P_{\mathcal{R}}(z_{t})F(z_{t})^{-1}\nabla f(z_{t})\|_{F(z_{t})}^{2}dt\leq\frac{2\Delta_{f}}{\chi\beta_{2}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int_{0}^{T}\eta_{t}\|P_{\mathcal{R}}(z_{t})F(z_{t})^{-1}\nabla f(z_{t})\|_{F(z_{t})}^{2}dt\leq\frac{2\Delta_{f}}{\chi\beta_{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int_{0}^{T}\eta_{t}\|s_{t}\|_{F(z_{t})}^{2}dt\leq\frac{2\Delta_{f}}{\chi\alpha}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int_{0}^{T}\eta_{t}\|s_{t}\|_{F(z_{t})}^{2}dt\leq\frac{2\Delta_{f}}{\chi\alpha}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S7.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 7.4. ‣ 7 Theoretical Analysis to Training Dynamics ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.4</span></a> demonstrates that increasing both <math id="S7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> yields a tighter integral bound for the Riemannian gradient on <math id="S7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> (<em id="S7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em> <math id="S7.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P_{\mathcal{R}}(z_{t})F(z_{t})^{-1}\nabla f(z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathcal{R}}(z_{t})F(z_{t})^{-1}\nabla f(z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>) and the momentum <math id="S7.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. This theoretical result validates the effectiveness of the strategy employed in <span id="S7.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LITE</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LITE</span>, a generalized strategy to accelerate LLM pre-training by enhancing training dynamics along flat directions. Experimentally, <span id="S8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LITE</span> significantly enhances state-of-the-art optimizers such as Muon and SOAP. Our results indicate that <span id="S8.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LITE</span> consistently achieves lower terminal loss and exhibits superior scaling behavior compared to corresponding baselines across various LLM pre-training tasks. For future work, we plan to explore adaptive mechanisms for dynamic hyper-parameter tuning within <span id="S8.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LITE</span>, and extending its integration with other emerging optimizers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Impact Statement</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper presents work whose goal is to advance the field of Machine
Learning, with a focus on understanding and improving the pre-training of LLMs. There are many potential societal consequences of our work, none
which we feel must be specifically highlighted here.</p>
</div>
</section>
