<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:345.0pt;height:1.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:173%;">Appendix<span id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:345.0pt;height:1.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></span></p>
</div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>More Related Works</h2>

<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Comparison with methods enhancing dynamics along flat directions.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent strategies accelerate LLM pre-training by assigning larger learning rates to flat components. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> adjust learning rates based on an element-wise diagonal Fisher approximation, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> apply block-wise learning rates to blocks identified as flat via averaged sharpness. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025</a>)</cite> further employs a matrix-level precision subspace detected via a lazy PCA-based estimator.
Compared to these methods, <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LITE</span> identifies flat subspaces with matrix-level precision using highly efficient numerical techniques that introduce no extra optimizer states.
Crucially, prior studies focus exclusively on learning rate adjustment, a strategy <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LITE</span> subsumes by simply setting <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, we <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">also address the dynamics of momentum</span> in anisotropic landscapes. We elucidate how momentum and preconditioners jointly influence training dynamics and <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">enhance momentum evolution</span> in flat directions by explicitly increasing Hessian damping.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Comparison with algorithmic frameworks for adaptive optimizers.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pethick et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bernstein and Newhouse, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2026</a>)</cite> interpret the update step of certain adaptive optimizers as the solution to a constrained optimization problem governed by operator norms:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle U^{*}=\operatorname*{argmin}_{\|U\|_{\operatorname{op}}\leq 1}\langle U,M\rangle," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">argmin</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>op</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle U^{*}=\operatorname*{argmin}_{\|U\|_{\operatorname{op}}\leq 1}\langle U,M\rangle,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the momentum at the current iterate, <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is the Frobenius inner product, and <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{\operatorname{op}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>op</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{\operatorname{op}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents a specific operator norm. This formulation formally unifies methods like Muon and Lion, offering insights into their stability (e.g., by controlling the magnitude of activations during propagation). However, a limitation is that the induced geometry is typically independent of the local curvature at the current iterate, making it difficult to encompass methods that decouple momentum and precondition updates like AdamW and SOAP, which rely on iterates-dependent preconditions. Furthermore, this perspective fails to justify the algorithmic necessity of accumulating momentum before preconditioning. In contrast, our proposed Riemannian ODE framework unifies a broader class of adaptive optimizers. It elucidates the joint role of momentum and preconditioners in shaping training dynamics and extends naturally to complex momentum schemes (see Appendix <a href="#A5.SS3" title="E.3 Modeling AdEMAMix via a Third-Order ODE ‣ Appendix E Discussions on Momentum Formulations and their Continuous-Time ODE Counterparts ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Algorithm Details</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Details of <span id="A2.SS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Muon-LITE</span></h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For Muon and <span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Muon-LITE</span>, we adopt the Newton-Schulz (NS) iteration termed Polar Express in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amsel et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and set
Newton–Schulz iterations to 6. This approach yields faster convergence to the theoretical optimum than the method in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jordan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the <span id="A2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">embedding, norm, output</span> blocks, we use the same methods as in <span id="A2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SOAP-LITE</span> with <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{l}=I,Q_{r}=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{l}=I,Q_{r}=I</annotation></semantics></math> to estimate the projection <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to sharp directions. See more details in <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Details of SOAP-LITE ‣ Appendix B Algorithm Details ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>. Next we introduce how to <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for other blocks using <span id="A2.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Muon-LITE</span>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Estimating <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> via Efficient Composite Newton-Schulz.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To efficiently estimate the sharp subspace projection <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> at iteration <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, we employ a lightweight composite Newton-Schulz (NS) scheme. The process involves two main steps: constructing a filtering operator and dynamically adjusting the threshold. For any matrix <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> with Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=U\Sigma V^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=U\Sigma V^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, We define <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{NS}(A)=A(A^{\top}A)^{-\frac{1}{2}}=UV^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>NS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{NS}(A)=A(A^{\top}A)^{-\frac{1}{2}}=UV^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the inverse denotes the Moore-Penrose pseudo-inverse.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1. Construction of the Filtering Operator.</span>
We first define a dynamic threshold <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}=l_{k}\|\widetilde{M}_{k}\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}=l_{k}\|\widetilde{M}_{k}\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a scaling factor. This threshold <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> serves to delineate the sharp subspace: specifically, we select the eigenspace of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}_{k}^{\top}\widetilde{M}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}_{k}^{\top}\widetilde{M}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> associated with eigenvalues larger than <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. The adaptive update mechanism for <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> (and thus <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>) will be detailed subsequently.
We then compute the filtering operator <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="A2.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}=\frac{1}{2}\operatorname{NS}(\widetilde{M}_{k})+\frac{1}{2}\operatorname{NS}\left(\frac{\widetilde{M}_{k}}{\tau_{k}}-\operatorname{NS}(\widetilde{M}_{k})\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>NS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>NS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>NS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}=\frac{1}{2}\operatorname{NS}(\widetilde{M}_{k})+\frac{1}{2}\operatorname{NS}\left(\frac{\widetilde{M}_{k}}{\tau_{k}}-\operatorname{NS}(\widetilde{M}_{k})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The projection matrix onto the sharp subspace is subsequently obtained by <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k}=T_{k}^{\top}T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k}=T_{k}^{\top}T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, the rationale for which is detailed in the subsequent parts ((<a href="#A2.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ Estimating 𝑃_𝑘 via Efficient Composite Newton-Schulz. ‣ B.1 Details of Muon-LITE ‣ Appendix B Algorithm Details ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) and (<a href="#A2.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ Estimating 𝑃_𝑘 via Efficient Composite Newton-Schulz. ‣ B.1 Details of Muon-LITE ‣ Appendix B Algorithm Details ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>)). It is worth noting that this method for estimating <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> necessitates relatively high precision from the NS function. Consequently, we employ the Polar Express method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amsel et al., 2025</a>)</cite> rather than the vanilla NS iterations used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jordan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2. Dynamic Sharp Subspace Dimension Adjustment.</span>
To ensure the rank of the projection <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> approximates the target dimension <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, we update the scaling factor <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> based on the effective rank (measured by <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|P_{k}\|_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|P_{k}\|_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
<table id="A2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle l_{k+1}=\begin{cases}1.05\cdot l_{k},&amp;\text{if }\|P_{k}\|_{F}\geq\sqrt{d_{s}},\\
0.95\cdot l_{k},&amp;\text{if }\|P_{k}\|_{F}&lt;\sqrt{d_{s}}.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1.05</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>0.95</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle l_{k+1}=\begin{cases}1.05\cdot l_{k},&amp;\text{if }\|P_{k}\|_{F}\geq\sqrt{d_{s}},\\
0.95\cdot l_{k},&amp;\text{if }\|P_{k}\|_{F}&lt;\sqrt{d_{s}}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">This feedback loop effectively maintains <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> near the <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>-th largest singular value of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theoretical Intuition.</span>
To understand why <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> acts as a subspace filter, consider the SVD of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}_{k}=U\Sigma V^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}_{k}=U\Sigma V^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let the singular values be ordered as <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1}\geq\dots\geq\sigma_{j}&gt;\tau_{k}&gt;\sigma_{j+1}\geq\dots\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1}\geq\dots\geq\sigma_{j}&gt;\tau_{k}&gt;\sigma_{j+1}\geq\dots\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the Newton-Schulz iteration converges to the matrix sign function, we have <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{NS}(\widetilde{M}_{k})=UV^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>NS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{NS}(\widetilde{M}_{k})=UV^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, the input to the second NS term becomes:</p>
<table id="A2.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\widetilde{M}_{k}}{\tau_{k}}-\operatorname{NS}(\widetilde{M}_{k})=U\operatorname{diag}\left(\frac{\sigma_{1}}{\tau_{k}}-1,\dots,\frac{\sigma_{r}}{\tau_{k}}-1\right)V^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>NS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\widetilde{M}_{k}}{\tau_{k}}-\operatorname{NS}(\widetilde{M}_{k})=U\operatorname{diag}\left(\frac{\sigma_{1}}{\tau_{k}}-1,\dots,\frac{\sigma_{r}}{\tau_{k}}-1\right)V^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Applying <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{NS}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>NS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{NS}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to (<a href="#A2.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ Estimating 𝑃_𝑘 via Efficient Composite Newton-Schulz. ‣ B.1 Details of Muon-LITE ‣ Appendix B Algorithm Details ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) maps positive entries (where <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}&gt;\tau_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}&gt;\tau_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>) to <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1</annotation></semantics></math> and negative entries (where <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}&lt;\tau_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}&lt;\tau_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>) to <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, we have</p>
<table id="A2.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}=\frac{1}{2}\operatorname{NS}(\widetilde{M}_{k})+\frac{1}{2}\operatorname{NS}\left(\frac{\widetilde{M}_{k}}{\tau_{k}}-\operatorname{NS}(\widetilde{M}_{k})\right)=U\operatorname{diag}(1,\dots,1,0,\dots,0)V^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>NS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>NS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>NS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>diag</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}=\frac{1}{2}\operatorname{NS}(\widetilde{M}_{k})+\frac{1}{2}\operatorname{NS}\left(\frac{\widetilde{M}_{k}}{\tau_{k}}-\operatorname{NS}(\widetilde{M}_{k})\right)=U\operatorname{diag}(1,\dots,1,0,\dots,0)V^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Computational Efficiency of </span><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Muon-LITE</span><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span> Overall, <span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Muon-LITE</span> requires only two additional NS functions per step compared to vanilla Muon, which can be executed in parallel. Since NS iterations constitute a nearly negligible fraction of the computational cost in large-batch pre-training (dominated by gradient computation), this slight overhead is virtually imperceptible in practice.
For example, training a LLaMA-1.3B model on the Pile dataset (global batch size 8192, micro batch size 16, sequence length 1024, on <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="8\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8\times</annotation></semantics></math> A800-80GB GPUs) yields a throughput of 100.4k tokens/s for <span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Muon-LITE</span> versus 101.5k tokens/s for Muon. This reflects a throughput drop of only <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 1\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, efficiency can be enhanced via system-level optimizations, such as kernel fusion for the NS iterations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Details of <span id="A2.SS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SOAP-LITE</span></h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="A2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SOAP-LITE</span>, we employ a dynamic thresholding strategy generating a smoothed projection onto the sharp directions to enhance training stability. To avoid the computational latency and memory overhead associated with exact <span id="A2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">top_k</span> operations in PyTorch, especially for large matrices, we approximate the top-<math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> thresholds via adaptive scalar variables, similar to the approach in <span id="A2.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Muon-LITE</span>.
We define <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> as the target dimension of the sharp subspace, and <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{smooth}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>smooth</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{smooth}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the dimension of the transitional subspace bridging the sharp and flat directions. We maintain two dynamic scalars, <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{k}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{k}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> (controlling the sharp boundary) and <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{k}^{\text{smooth}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext>smooth</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{k}^{\text{smooth}}</annotation></semantics></math> (controlling the smooth boundary), initialized as <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{0}^{s}=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{0}^{s}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{0}^{\text{smooth}}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mn>0</mn><mtext>smooth</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{0}^{\text{smooth}}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At the <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th iteration, we estimate the thresholds for the top-<math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and top-<math id="A2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d_{s}+d_{\text{smooth}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>smooth</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d_{s}+d_{\text{smooth}})</annotation></semantics></math> elements based on the mean magnitude of the tensor <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}^{s}=l_{k}^{s}\operatorname{mean}(V_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}^{s}=l_{k}^{s}\operatorname{mean}(V_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}^{\text{smooth}}=l_{k}^{\text{smooth}}\operatorname{mean}(V_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext>smooth</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext>smooth</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}^{\text{smooth}}=l_{k}^{\text{smooth}}\operatorname{mean}(V_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>. The smoothed projection matrix <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is then constructed element-wise as:</p>
<table id="A2.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{k})_{i,j}=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if }(V_{k})_{i,j}\geq\tau_{k}^{s},\\[6.0pt]
\displaystyle\frac{(V_{k})_{i,j}-\tau_{k}^{\text{smooth}}}{\tau_{k}^{s}-\tau_{k}^{\text{smooth}}},&amp;\text{if }\tau_{k}^{\text{smooth}}\leq(V_{k})_{i,j}&lt;\tau_{k}^{s},\\[6.0pt]
0,&amp;\text{if }(V_{k})_{i,j}\leq\tau_{k}^{\text{smooth}}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext>smooth</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext>smooth</mtext></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext>smooth</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext>smooth</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{k})_{i,j}=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if }(V_{k})_{i,j}\geq\tau_{k}^{s},\\[6.0pt]
\displaystyle\frac{(V_{k})_{i,j}-\tau_{k}^{\text{smooth}}}{\tau_{k}^{s}-\tau_{k}^{\text{smooth}}},&amp;\text{if }\tau_{k}^{\text{smooth}}\leq(V_{k})_{i,j}&lt;\tau_{k}^{s},\\[6.0pt]
0,&amp;\text{if }(V_{k})_{i,j}\leq\tau_{k}^{\text{smooth}}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Then we dynamically adjust the coefficients <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{k}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{k}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{k}^{\text{smooth}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext>smooth</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{k}^{\text{smooth}}</annotation></semantics></math> based on the current sparsity levels:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle l_{k+1}^{s}=\begin{cases}1.05\cdot l_{k}^{s},&amp;|\{V_{i,j}:V_{ij}&gt;\tau_{k}^{s}\}|\geq d_{s},\\
0.95\cdot l_{k}^{s},&amp;|\{V_{i,j}:V_{i,j}&gt;\tau_{k}^{s}\}|&lt;d_{s},\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1.05</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>0.95</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle l_{k+1}^{s}=\begin{cases}1.05\cdot l_{k}^{s},&amp;|\{V_{i,j}:V_{ij}&gt;\tau_{k}^{s}\}|\geq d_{s},\\
0.95\cdot l_{k}^{s},&amp;|\{V_{i,j}:V_{i,j}&gt;\tau_{k}^{s}\}|&lt;d_{s},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="A2.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle l_{k+1}^{\text{smooth}}=\begin{cases}1.05\cdot l_{k}^{\text{smooth}},&amp;|\{V_{i,j}:V_{ij}&gt;\tau_{k}^{\text{smooth}}\}|\geq d_{s}+d_{\text{smooth}},\\
0.95\cdot l_{k}^{\text{smooth}},&amp;|\{V_{i,j}:V_{i,j}&gt;\tau_{k}^{\text{smooth}}\}|&lt;d_{s}+d_{\text{smooth}},\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mtext>smooth</mtext></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1.05</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext>smooth</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext>smooth</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>smooth</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>0.95</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext>smooth</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext>smooth</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>smooth</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle l_{k+1}^{\text{smooth}}=\begin{cases}1.05\cdot l_{k}^{\text{smooth}},&amp;|\{V_{i,j}:V_{ij}&gt;\tau_{k}^{\text{smooth}}\}|\geq d_{s}+d_{\text{smooth}},\\
0.95\cdot l_{k}^{\text{smooth}},&amp;|\{V_{i,j}:V_{i,j}&gt;\tau_{k}^{\text{smooth}}\}|&lt;d_{s}+d_{\text{smooth}},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|\cdot|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\cdot|</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the cardinality of the set. Finally, we enforce the constraint <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{k+1}^{\text{smooth}}\leftarrow\min\{0.95l_{k+1}^{s},l_{k+1}^{\text{smooth}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mtext>smooth</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.95</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mtext>smooth</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{k+1}^{\text{smooth}}\leftarrow\min\{0.95l_{k+1}^{s},l_{k+1}^{\text{smooth}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure that the smoothing threshold remains strictly lower than the sharp threshold (<math id="A2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{k}^{\text{smooth}}&lt;l_{k}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext>smooth</mtext></msubsup><mo>&lt;</mo><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{k}^{\text{smooth}}&lt;l_{k}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math>). Our approach eliminates the use of inefficient top-<math id="A2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> functions, thereby incurring almost no additional computational or temporal burden.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Experimental Details</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Models.</span> We utilize two popular classes of LLM models for our pre-training experiments:</p>
<ul id="A3.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:2em;">
<li id="A3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLaMA.</span> LLaMA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023a</a>)</cite> is a widely adopted dense decoder-only Transformer architecture. It employs Rotary Positional Embeddings (RoPE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Su et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Swish-Gated Linear Units (SwiGLU), and Root Mean Square Layer Normalization (RMSNorm). In this work, we pre-train LLaMA models with sizes ranging from 130M to 1.3B parameters. Detailed model configurations are provided in Table <a href="#A3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Appendix C Experimental Details ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">QwenMoE.</span> Qwen2MoE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> is a prominent open-source Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) decoder-only Transformer. In contrast to LLaMA, Qwen2MoE integrates a hybrid attention mechanism (combining sliding window and full attention) alongside its MoE architecture. For our experiments, we disable sliding window attention given the relatively short context length. We configure the model to activate 4 experts per token. Auxiliary losses including <math id="A3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-loss with coefficient 0.001 and load balancing loss with coefficient 0.01 are used to make sure stable training. Refer to Table <a href="#A3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Appendix C Experimental Details ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> for comprehensive configuration details.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T2" class="ltx_table">
