<td id="A3.T3.6.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;"><span id="A3.T3.6.2.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">15</span></td>
<td id="A3.T3.6.2.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;"><span id="A3.T3.6.2.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">32</span></td>
<td id="A3.T3.6.2.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;"><span id="A3.T3.6.2.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">2e-3</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct pre-training on the following datasets:</p>
<ul id="A3.I2" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:2em;">
<li id="A3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C4</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raffel et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. The Colossal Clean Crawled Corpus (C4) dataset is employed for our small-to-medium scale pre-training experiments. We use the T5 tokenizer with a vocabulary size of 32,100.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Pile</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. We utilize The Pile for large-scale (or larger-context) pre-training tasks. For this dataset, we adopt the LLaMA-2 tokenizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023b</a>)</cite> with a vocabulary size of 32,000.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sequence Packing/Batching.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the Pile dataset, we employ a standard <em id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sequence packing</em> strategy: documents are tokenized, concatenated into a continuous stream, and then segmented into fixed-length sequences of size <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> (depending on the dataset). This approach minimizes padding overhead and ensures a near-constant number of effective tokens per batch. Training progress is measured in terms of total tokens, with evaluation performed on the official validation splits.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LR schedulers.</span>
We evaluate two popular learning-rate (LR) scheduling strategies with 1,000 warm-up steps in all pre-training experiments:</p>
<ul id="A3.I3" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:2em;">
<li id="A3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cos</span> (cosine scheduler) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>:
a linear warm-up to peak <span id="A3.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">lr_max</span>, followed by cosine decay to a terminal LR <span id="A3.I3.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">lr_min</span>.
Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoffmann et al. (2022)</a></cite>, we set <math id="A3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{lr\_min}=0.1\times\texttt{lr\_max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">lr_min</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">lr_max</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{lr\_min}=0.1\times\texttt{lr\_max}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">wsd</span> (warmup-stable-decay scheduler) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hägele et al., 2024</a>)</cite>:
a linear warm-up to <span id="A3.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">lr_max</span>, followed by a stable phase keeping LR at <span id="A3.I3.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">lr_max</span> (up to 80% of total steps), and then a linear decay to 0 over the final 20% steps.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Experimental Details in Sections <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Practical Implementation of LITE ‣ 5 The LITE Approach ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> </h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection, we demonstrate that gradients (first-order information) can effectively approximate the top eigenspaces of the Hessian (second-order information) for matrix block parameters in language models. Similar alignment phenomena have been extensively investigated and verified in simplified models, both theoretically and empirically <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Wu, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. To validate this in our context, we conduct a experiment on a small LLAMA2 model with <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\operatorname{model}}=16,d_{\operatorname{model}}=32,\text{n\_head}=4,\text{depth}=4,\text{vocabulary size}=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>n_head</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>depth</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>vocabulary size</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\operatorname{model}}=16,d_{\operatorname{model}}=32,\text{n\_head}=4,\text{depth}=4,\text{vocabulary size}=8</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\text{params}=10,640" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>params</mtext></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10,640</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\text{params}=10,640</annotation></semantics></math>. The model is trained using AdamW for 100 steps with a token batch size <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8\times 8=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8\times 8=64</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{lr}=0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>lr</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{lr}=0.001</annotation></semantics></math>. For Q,K,V,O, FFN (up, gate, down) blocks, we compute the Hessian eigenvalue distribution within each block (<a href="#A3.F8" title="In C.1 Experimental Details in Sections ‣ Appendix C Experimental Details ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figures</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Training Dynamics and Loss Landscape in Deep Learning. ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), test the alignment degree of each row’s Hessian with <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\top}G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\top}G</annotation></semantics></math>, and each column’s Hessian with <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GG^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GG^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#A3.F7" title="In C.1 Experimental Details in Sections ‣ Appendix C Experimental Details ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figures</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Training Dynamics and Loss Landscape in Deep Learning. ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Accelerating LLM Pre-Training through Flat-Direction Dynamics Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="A3.F7.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:158.7pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22681v1/model.layers.1.mlp.down_proj.weight.svg" id="A3.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:219/71;" width="219" height="71"></object>
